Hi Zhichen,

Thanks for posting the question. I don't think your intended use of if-then will work. The model language is declarative, and the graphical relationships among nodes (what depends on what) must be fixed and known when the model is built. Although your "sign" vector is in constants, it won't work as written. When nimble sees a declaration inside a for-loop, it creates the entire set of graphical relationships from the indices in the declaration's expression. It does not work through statements inside for-loops one by one in an imperative manner (what most people are used to in most programming languages).

nimble does support definition-time if-then-else, but only at the outermost level (not inside a for loop). And that is equivalent to including only one section of code or another when the model is created, not including it for some values of some indices.

A possible way to do what you want is as follows:
 for (i in 1:num_ones) {
   we[ones[i]] <- a[ones[i], 1]/a[ones[i[], 2]
}
for(i in 1:num_zeros) {
  we[zeros[i]] <- 0
}

where you need to create the index vectors "ones" and "zeros" in R and provide them as part of the constants list.

HTH
Perry

